blocks.text:
Investigate third parties (suppliers, vendors, commission agents, brokers, sales representatives, distributors, contractors, customs brokers, consultants, and all other business partners) to ensure anti-corruption compliance and prevent third-party corruption incidents, in accordance with applicable laws including the Prevention of Corruption Act, 1988 (as amended in 2018), the Companies Act, 2013, and the UN Convention against Corruption;
Investigate third parties to ensure information security compliance and prevent third-party information security incidents, in accordance with ISO/IEC 27001:2022 (Controls A.5.19, A.5.20, A.5.21, A.5.22, A.5.23) and the CERT-In Comprehensive Cyber Security Audit Policy Guidelines (2025);
Operationalise a risk-based approach to third-party due diligence, underpinned by systematic data collection and benchmarking against industry standards; and
Embed contractual protections, ongoing monitoring, and audit rights to detect and mitigate compliance failures throughout the lifecycle of each third-party relationship.

body.text:
Third party requests payment to a bank account in a different country from the one in which it operates;
Third party refuses to provide complete ownership information or references;
Third party has been named in any regulatory enforcement action (SEBI, CBI, ED, Interpol);
Third party has pending litigations for fraud, corruption, or money laundering;
Third party is located in a jurisdiction with high corruption risk, as per Transparency International's Corruption Perceptions Index (CPI) or the World Bank's Control of Corruption indicator;
Third party is related to (or recommended by) a Gautam Solar employee (potential conflict of interest);
Third party requests that payments be made in cash, through offshore accounts, or to a third-party beneficiary;
Third party insists on unusual confidentiality terms that would prevent Gautam Solar from conducting compliance checks.

Beneficial ownership verification – ownership structure chart identifying all individuals with 10% or more direct or indirect ownership interest;
Adverse media screening – search of publicly available databases, news archives, and regulatory enforcement records for the third party entity and each principal;
Background verification – for agents, sales representatives, and consultants, Gautam Solar may engage an external due diligence provider (e.g., Kroll, Exiger, local Indian equivalent) to conduct enhanced background checks;
Site visit – physical or virtual (video) inspection of the third party's office and operations;
Government official check – confirmation that no principal holds a position as a public official (or, if so, a detailed risk assessment and mitigation plan approved by the Compliance Committee).

Third-party name, type, risk tier, and date of risk assessment;
Compliance scores for each data element collected;
Any red flags identified and the rationale for acceptance/rejection;
Approval sign-off and date;
Date of next scheduled monitoring review.

Specific measures to reduce the risk (e.g., reduced scope, quarterly compliance certifications, escrow payment arrangements);
Enhanced monitoring frequency (e.g., monthly instead of quarterly);
Exit triggers – conditions under which Gautam Solar will terminate the relationship.

Anti-corruption representation and warranty – third party confirms compliance with all applicable anti-corruption laws;
Audit clause – Gautam Solar reserves the right to audit third-party books and records relevant to the engagement, at Gautam Solar's expense, with reasonable notice;
Termination for cause – material breach of the anti-corruption provisions entitles Gautam Solar to terminate the contract immediately without liability;
Reporting obligation – third party must immediately report to Gautam Solar any request for improper payments or any actual or suspected violation of anti-corruption laws in connection with Gautam Solar's business;
Subcontractor flow-down – if third party subcontracts any part of the work, the same anti-corruption obligations must be flowed down to the subcontractor, and third party remains fully liable.

Data processing agreement (DPA) – compliant with applicable data protection laws (including India's Digital Personal Data Protection Act, 2023, if applicable);
Confidentiality and non-disclosure obligations – surviving termination;
Security schedule – attaching the information security questionnaire responses as a contractual commitment (not merely informative);
Incident notification – third party must notify Gautam Solar within 24 hours of discovery of any security incident affecting Gautam Solar data (exceeding CERT-In's 6-hour requirement for high-risk data);
Data return and deletion – upon termination, third party must return all Gautam Solar data and provide certification of secure deletion;
Subcontractor flow-down – any subcontractor handling Gautam Solar data must be bound by equivalent information security obligations, and third party must provide a list of all subcontractors (which must be pre-approved by Gautam Solar for high-risk data).
